### Step 9 — Close-out

**Close-out is an institution, not a courtesy.** Every finding this run's rigor machinery surfaced — walk, matrix discharge, auditor, critic — gets exactly one terminal disposition. "Continuation candidate" is abolished; nothing leaves this step homeless.

<!-- INJECT: terminal-disposition -->

The vehicle is a single-turn close-out report, sent to the user at Step 9 and never re-run as ceremony: plain English, at the altitude of decisions rather than of code, covering ten parts — goal, accomplished, deferred-with-dispositions (each finding's DO-NOW / ACCEPT-CLOSED / PROMOTE named), special attention, material risks, challenges, decisions, success/failure verdict, learnings, next. Authoring detail per part and the anti-ceremony bound live in `operational-rules.md`. Write `continuation.md` alongside it — the report is what the user reads; the file is what the next wave opens. Every run owes one act of consolidation, or a stated none: the run's debts (`session-poker.sh debt list`) show one burned by a row of this run, and failing that the continuation carries one line under `## Deferrals` reading `consolidation: none found — <reason>`. Under its `## Deferrals` heading close-out writes one line per open deferral, `deferred: <record>#<n> <S> <reach> "<title>" stated="<sentence|->" from=<wave name>`; the deferral's `finding-move` carries the changelog sentence it owes, and `release-check` prints `deferral <record>#<n>: stated` or `unstated` for each. Where a continuation already carries a `## Deferrals` heading, close-out adds under it, once each and after anything a person wrote there, every deferral of this run and every inherited one that is not already there, and leaves the file as it stands only when all of them are.

**Where the run ends.** Every close-out gives step 9 its `delivered=`, the work's terminal state: a PR open for a human to review, or commits landed locally and ready to push. Past that boundary is the human's process, and a run that claims it has overstated what it did.

Only where `deploy_target` names a live surface this run operates does step 9 also owe `deployed`, `verified` and `monitored`: the release lands, is verified on the surface, and is watched for one cycle before the report claims done.

**Archiving.** Once the run is delivered, close-out calls `archive_run` (`payload/scripts/lib/archive.sh`) with the closing plan's own directory under `<docs-root>/plans/`: an epic close or a standalone run's close moves that directory's `specs:`/`plans:`/`adrs:` trio to `<archive-root>/<project>/.bionic/<same relative path>`, and a wave inside an open epic moves nothing. `archive-on-close: false` in `.bionic/config.yaml` turns this off for the project. Close-out gives step 9 an `archived=` naming what moved, or why nothing did — the line `archive_run` itself printed.

